<Text index="0">
To update the value of `\fontscale` for a specific figure, you can use the `\resizebox` command from the `graphicx` package. Here's how you can do it:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{graphicx}

% Define the default font scale
\def\fontscale{0.9}

\begin{document}

% Use the default font scale
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{\fontscale * {your_image.png}}

% Use a different font scale for a specific figure
\resizebox{.9 * \fontscale\textwidth}{!}{\includegraphics{your_image.png}}

\end{document}
```

In the first `\includegraphics` command, the image is scaled using the default `\fontscale` value of `0.9`.

In the second `\resizebox` command, the image is scaled using a custom scaling factor of `0.9 * \fontscale`. This means that the final scaling factor will be `0.81` (0.9 multiplied by 0.9).

The `!` in the `\resizebox` command ensures that the aspect ratio of the image is maintained during the resizing.

You can adjust the scaling factor as needed for each figure by modifying the value inside the `\resizebox` command.
</Text>

